## PIN codes [Ex 1.5]{.tag}

A bank card has a four-digit PIN.

| Rule | Choices per position | Count | Kind of count |
|---|---|---|---|
| any digits | $10\cdot10\cdot10\cdot10$ | $10^4=10\,000$ | ordered, with repetition |
| all digits different | $10\cdot9\cdot8\cdot7$ | $5040$ | ordered, no repetition |

. . .

A thief who guesses one PIN at random hits it with probability [$1/10\,000$]{.answer}.

. . .

With repeats, each digit is chosen afresh and the product rule alone gives $n^k$, here $10^4$. Without repeats the choices shrink by one at each step. That case, and two related ones, have classical names.

## Permutation [Def 1.6]{.tag} {.smaller}

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="52%"}
::: {.box-def}
A **permutation** of $n$ distinct objects is an ordering of all of them in a row. There are $n!$ permutations, read "$n$ factorial":
$$n!=n(n-1)\cdots2\cdot1,\qquad 0!=1.$$
:::

. . .

Kemal drives a delivery van and has 5 shops to visit this morning. Each route is a permutation of the 5 shops:
$$5!=5\cdot4\cdot3\cdot2\cdot1=\color{#b4542a}{\mathbf{120}}\ \text{routes.}$$
:::
::: {.column width="48%"}

