## Arrangement [Def 1.7]{.tag} {.smaller}

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="42%"}

::: {#44ce6bff .cell fig-height='3.4' fig-width='5' execution_count=23}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![](L01-probability-basics_files/figure-revealjs/cell-24-output-1.png){width=717 height=477}
:::
:::


:::
::: {.column width="58%"}
::: {.box-def}
An **arrangement** of $k$ objects from $n$ distinct objects ($k\le n$) is an ordered selection without repetition. The number of arrangements is
$$\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}=n(n-1)\cdots(n-k+1).$$
:::

. . .

A supermarket puts 3 of its 12 tea brands into the eye-level slots: [1320]{.answer} arrangements. The PINs with four different digits are arrangements of 4 from 10: $10!/6!=5040$.

. . .

[With $k=n$ we get a permutation, and the formula gives $n!/0!=n!$. That is one reason to set $0!=1$.]{.muted}
:::
::::

## Combination [Def 1.8]{.tag}

::: {.box-def}
A **combination** of $k$ objects from $n$ distinct objects is an unordered selection without repetition, that is, a subset of size $k$. Their number is written $\binom{n}{k}$. We read it "$n$ choose $k$" and call it the binomial coefficient.
:::

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="50%"}
. . .

The same supermarket picks 3 of its 12 tea brands for a weekend discount. Now the three brands form a set, and nothing is first or last: $\binom{12}{3}=220$ ways.

. . .

The lottery and the inspector's sample, coming up, are combinations too.
:::
::: {.column width="50%"}

