## Other names and symbols

Other textbooks use other words and symbols for the same things, and you should recognise them.

| In this course | Also called | Symbols in other books |
|---|---|---|
| arrangement of $k$ from $n$ | $k$-permutation, partial permutation, variation | $A_n^k$, $P(n,k)$, ${}_nP_k$ |
| combination, $\binom{n}{k}$ | subset of size $k$ | $C_n^k$, ${}_nC_k$ |

. . .

::: {.box-ex}
Be careful with the word "arrangement" in English books. Many of them use it loosely for any ordering, a full permutation included. In this course it always means an ordered selection of $k$ from $n$.
:::

::: {.notes}
Mention $A_n^k$ and $C_n^k$ out loud for students who did school maths in Russian.
:::

## Orderings and selections [Thm 1.4]{.tag}

::: {.box-thm}
From $n$ distinct objects:

|  | permutation | arrangement | combination |
|:----------|:-----:|:-----:|:-------:|
| order matters? | yes | yes | no |
| how many used? | all $n$ | $k$ | $k$ |
| number | $n!$ | $\dfrac{n!}{(n-k)!}$ | $\dbinom{n}{k}=\dfrac{n!}{k!\,(n-k)!}$ |
:::

. . .

Each combination appears $k!$ times among the arrangements, once for each order:
$$\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}=k!\binom{n}{k},\qquad\text{tea: } 1320=3!\cdot220.$$

::: {.notes}
Board: three columns headed permutation, arrangement, combination. Under each write "order matters?" (yes, yes, no), "how many used?" (n, k, k) and the formula. Leave it up for the rest of the counting section.
:::

## Two facts about $\binom{n}{k}$ [Thm 1.4]{.tag}

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="45%"}
$$\binom{n}{k}=\binom{n}{n-k}$$
Choosing who goes in is the same as choosing who stays out.

. . .

$$\binom{n}{0}=\binom{n}{n}=1$$
One way to take nobody, one way to take everybody.
:::
::: {.column width="55%"}

